Chapter: EffectWidenScope

Section: Body

 A function that performs less than it was asked for is safe to hand over,
 and a scope is one of the ways it can perform less.

 `scoped-print` writes only to the stdout channel and says so. A parameter
 declared `Text -> [Console] Nothing` promises its caller only that it may
 do something Console-ish, and writing to stdout is something Console-ish.
 The argument boundary used to refuse this (CDX2090) because it widened
 only over the dot: a scoped label was left exactly as it was, so
 `[Console "stdout"]` never became `[Console]` and the rows never met.

 Dropping a scope looks like it should need to know what a scope MEANS --
 a filesystem scope is a path prefix, a network scope an authority, a
 console scope a channel, and no single test covers those. It does not,
 and that is the whole point: the target label here carries NO scope, so
 it states no restriction, and everything falls under no restriction.
 There is nothing to choose between. Asking whether "/config/x" falls
 under "/config/" is the other relation, it does need the family tag, and
 it is still refused -- see errors/effect-widen-scope-cross.

 Nothing is laundered by this. The two directions that would launder ship
 as errors/effect-widen-scope-deny (unscoped argument into a scoped
 parameter -- it may write anywhere and the callee believes otherwise) and
 errors/effect-widen-scope-cross (one scope for a different one). Both
 must stay refused; this file and those two only mean something together.

  scoped-print : Text -> [Console "stdout"] Nothing
  scoped-print (t) = act
    print-line-uni t
  end

  apply-it : (Text -> [Console] Nothing), Text -> [Console] Nothing
  apply-it (f) (t) = act
    f t
  end

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    apply-it scoped-print "widened"
  end
